{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016fdiyyzYYKRGfxfjDJkBSp","type":"tool_result","content":"def witness_layouts(matrix, ordering) -> tuple[list[int], list[int]]:\n    \"\"\"The two customer layouts a pattern ordering induces: by opening time\n    (position of the first pattern a customer needs, ties by index) and by\n    closing time (last pattern). The *opening* layout has vertex separation at\n    most the ordering's open-stack count minus one (a placed vertex with a\n    neighbour outside the prefix is open at the next opening position, and so\n    is the vertex opening there), so its prefixes seed the search with sets\n    witnessing `fhat(t) <= t + pw`. The closing layout carries no such\n    guarantee under the prefix definition (the cost of a layout is not\n    reversal-invariant; `vertex_separation_of` on it is recorded, not relied\n    on) and its finished sets are seeds like any other.\"\"\"\n    matrix = np.asarray(matrix, dtype=int)\n    n = matrix.shape[0]\n    pos = {p: i for i, p in enumerate(ordering)}\n    first, last = [], []\n    for v in range(n):\n        pats = [pos[p] for p in np.flatnonzero(matrix[v]) if p in pos]\n        first.append(min(pats) if pats else 0)\n        last.append(max(pats) if pats else 0)\n    opening = sorted(range(n), key=lambda v: (first[v], v))\n    closing = sorted(range(n), key=lambda v: (last[v], v))\n    return opening, closing\n\n\ndef solution_record(name: str) -> dict | None:\n    from satisfiability.mosp_solver import _solution_path\n    from mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n\n    path = _solution_path(MOSPInstance.from_matrix([[1]], name=name), SOLUTIONS_DIR)\n    if not path.exists():\n        return None\n    return json.loads(path.read_text())\n\n\ndef safe_name(name: str) -> str:\n    return \"\".join(c if c.isalnum() or c in \"-_.\" else \"_\" for c in (name or \"unnamed\"))\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the profile: exact and searched\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n@dataclass\ndef load_targets() -> dict:\n    return json.loads(TARGETS_JSON.read_text())\n\n\ndef target_names(which: str, targets: dict | None = None) -> list[str]:\n    targets = targets or load_targets()\n    if which == \"all\":\n        names: list[str] = []\n        seen = set()\n        for key in (\"gap338\", \"cs200\", \"rest500\", \"small\"):\n            for nm in targets[key]:\n                if nm not in seen:\n                    seen.add(nm)\n                    names.append(nm)\n        return names\n    if which not in targets or which == \"open24\":\n        raise SystemExit(f\"unknown or unsupported target set {which!r}\")\n    return list(targets[which])\n\n\ndef ledger() -> pd.DataFrame:\n    return pd.read_csv(LEDGER_CORPUS)\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# stage: ceiling\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef _ceiling_job(args) -> dict:\ndef matrix_sha256(matrix) -> str:\n    \"\"\"SHA-256 of the 0/1 rows as the standard `.mosp` format writes them.\"\"\"\n    matrix = np.asarray(matrix, dtype=int)\n    text = \"\".join(\" \".join(str(int(v)) for v in row) + \"\\n\" for row in matrix)\n    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()\n\n\ndef write_standard_instance(path: Path, name: str, matrix) -> None:\n    matrix = np.asarray(matrix, dtype=int)\n    lines = [name, f\"{matrix.shape[0]} {matrix.shape[1]}\"]\n    lines += [\" \".join(str(int(v)) for v in row) for row in matrix]\n    path.write_text(\"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef _drat_trim_path() -> Path:\n    from learning.proofs import DRAT_TRIM\n----LEDGER\n70:LEDGER_CORPUS = OUT / \"ledger_corpus.csv\"\n71-HARPER_CSV = OUT / \"harper12.csv\"\n72-LEDGER_DATASET = OUT / \"ledger_dataset.csv.gz\"\n73:TARGETS_JSON = OUT / \"targets.json\"\n74-TABLES_MD = OUT / \"tables.md\"\n75-RUN_LOG = OUT / \"run.log\"\n76-VIOLATION_MD = OUT / \"VIOLATION.md\"\n77-\n78-# The harness's seven size bands (`learning.bound_harness.BANDS`), copied so that\n79-# this module does not import networkx at load; the test pins the two equal.\n80:BANDS = ((9, 20), (21, 30), (31, 40), (41, 60), (61, 75), (76, 100), (101, 134))\n81-HARPER_CAP = 12\n82-HARPER_BUDGET = 60.0\n83-SMALL_N = 22\n84-REST_SIZE = 500\n85-REST_SEED = 0\n86-DATASET_MAX_COMPONENT = 1024\n87-CLIQUE_BUDGET = 2.0\n88-CLIQUE_CAP = 200_000\n--\n142:def adjacency_from_matrix(matrix: np.ndarray) -> list[int]:\n143-    \"\"\"The MOSP graph of a 0/1 matrix (rows customers): a clique per pattern.\"\"\"\n144-    matrix = np.asarray(matrix)\n145-    n = matrix.shape[0]\n146-    adj = [0] * n\n147-    for col in range(matrix.shape[1]):\n148-        holders = np.flatnonzero(matrix[:, col])\n149-        together = 0\n150-        for c in holders:\n--\n342:def corpus_instances() -> dict[str, tuple[Path, object]]:\n343-    from learning.dataset import enumerate_instances\n344-\n345-    found: dict[str, tuple[Path, object]] = {}\n346-    for path, inst in enumerate_instances():\n347-        found.setdefault(inst.name, (path, inst))\n348-    return found\n349-\n350-\n----HARNESS\nsource\ncampaign    37800\ncorpus       6376\nupward       5686\nName: count, dtype: int64\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 5, in <module>\nTypeError: object of type 'int' has no len()\n----REPORT\n807:### 3.2 Numbers\n924:### 3.3 Through the harness\n939:### 3.4 Kill verdict and compute\n966:### 3.5 What this does not claim\n989:## 4. Family M by search: the contraction-sequence search, its certificate and checker; the 338 at `n ≤ 60` against the greedy ladder (item 04)\n1012:### 4.1 What was done\n1125:### 4.2 Numbers\n1192:### 4.3 Kill verdict and what this does not claim\n1231:### 4.4 Tables (generated; `tables.md`)\n1233:## (a) Per band: the ladder's floor gap and the search's (optimum units)\n12